      "layering_notes": "Santa Cruz County housing programs sit in the Community Development and Infrastructure Department (CDI) Planning Division, Housing Section (HousingProgramsInfo@santacruzcountyCA.gov, (831) 454-2332), which runs the Measure J inclusionary program, the Affordable Housing Impact Fee, LMIHAF, PLHA and the County housing NOFA; the Human Services Department's Housing for Health Division (H4H, the CoC CA-508 lead) runs HHAP, Homekey and homeless programs; Behavioral Health holds BHSA; and the Housing Authority of the County of Santa Cruz (HACSC) supplies vouchers. The County is a non-entitlement jurisdiction for CDBG and HOME. County programs apply only to the unincorporated area (about half the population); Santa Cruz, Watsonville, Capitola and Scotts Valley run their own programs (Pass 3). cdi.santacruzcountyca.gov, santacruzcountyca.gov PDFs, santacruzhealth.org and housingforhealthpartnership.org open normally; the Board's IQM2 portal (santacruzcountyca.iqm2.com) renders by script and could not be listed, so Board items were taken from NOFA appendices and press coverage. Santa Cruz will not use BHSA for capital; its value to a developer is a 15 to 20 year project-based rental or operating subsidy on PSH units, which can replace or supplement PBVs while HACSC's RFP is paused. Source is the revised June 2026 Integrated Plan PDF on santacruzhealth.org. Audit reclassification: funding_type set to grant because the plan funds rental subsidies ($6,539,529), bundled rental and operating subsidies for permanent settings ($914,623) and bundled interim housing subsidies ($781,451) a year; the plan answers No to BHSA capital development, and 0% of subsidies are administered through flex pools in the budget tables.",
